/*!

@page build_guide Building applications

@tableofcontents

This is about compiling and linking applications that use Lumina.  For information on
how to write such applications, start with the
[introductory tutorial](@ref quick_guide).  For information on how to compile
the Lumina library itself, see @ref compile_guide.

This is not a tutorial on compilation or linking.  It assumes basic
understanding of how to compile and link a C program as well as how to use the
specific compiler of your chosen development environment.  The compilation
and linking process should be explained in your C programming material and in
the documentation for your development environment.


@section build_include Including the Lumina header file

You should include the Lumina header in the source files where you use OpenGL or
Lumina.

@code
#include <Lumina/lumina.h>
@endcode

This header defines all the constants and declares all the types and function
prototypes of the Lumina API.  By default it also includes the OpenGL header from
your development environment.  See [option macros](@ref build_macros) below for
how to select OpenGL ES headers and more.

The Lumina header also defines any platform-specific macros needed by your OpenGL
header, so that it can be included without needing any window system headers.

It does this only when needed, so if window system headers are included, the
Lumina header does not try to redefine those symbols.  The reverse is not true,
i.e. `windows.h` cannot cope if any Win32 symbols have already been defined.

In other words:

 - Use the Lumina header to include OpenGL or OpenGL ES headers portably
 - Do not include window system headers unless you will use those APIs directly
 - If you do need such headers, include them before the Lumina header

If you are using an OpenGL extension loading library such as
[glad](https://github.com/Dav1dde/glad), the extension loader header should
be included before the Lumina one.  Lumina attempts to detect any OpenGL or OpenGL
ES header or extension loader header included before it and will then disable
the inclusion of the default OpenGL header.  Most extension loaders also define
macros that disable similar headers below it.

@code
#include <glad/gl.h>
#include <Lumina/lumina.h>
@endcode

Both of these mechanisms depend on the extension loader header defining a known
macro.  If yours doesn't or you don't know which one your users will pick, the
@ref LUM_INCLUDE_NONE macro will explicitly to prevent the Lumina header from
including the OpenGL header.  This will also allow you to include the two
headers in any order.

@code
#define LUM_INCLUDE_NONE
#include <Lumina/lumina.h>
#include <glad/gl.h>
@endcode


@subsection build_macros Lumina header option macros

These macros may be defined before the inclusion of the Lumina header and affect
its behavior.

@anchor LUM_DLL
__LUM_DLL__ is required on Windows when using the Lumina DLL, to tell the
compiler that the Lumina functions are defined in a DLL.

The following macros control which OpenGL or OpenGL ES API header is included.
Only one of these may be defined at a time.

@note Lumina does not provide any of the API headers mentioned below.  They are
provided by your development environment or your OpenGL, OpenGL ES or Vulkan
SDK, and most of them can be downloaded from the
[Khronos Registry](https://www.khronos.org/registry/).

@anchor LUM_INCLUDE_GLCOREARB
__LUM_INCLUDE_GLCOREARB__ makes the Lumina header include the modern
`GL/glcorearb.h` header (`OpenGL/gl3.h` on macOS) instead of the regular OpenGL
header.

@anchor LUM_INCLUDE_ES1
__LUM_INCLUDE_ES1__ makes the Lumina header include the OpenGL ES 1.x `GLES/gl.h`
header instead of the regular OpenGL header.

@anchor LUM_INCLUDE_ES2
__LUM_INCLUDE_ES2__ makes the Lumina header include the OpenGL ES 2.0
`GLES2/gl2.h` header instead of the regular OpenGL header.

@anchor LUM_INCLUDE_ES3
__LUM_INCLUDE_ES3__ makes the Lumina header include the OpenGL ES 3.0
`GLES3/gl3.h` header instead of the regular OpenGL header.

@anchor LUM_INCLUDE_ES31
__LUM_INCLUDE_ES31__ makes the Lumina header include the OpenGL ES 3.1
`GLES3/gl31.h` header instead of the regular OpenGL header.

@anchor LUM_INCLUDE_ES32
__LUM_INCLUDE_ES32__ makes the Lumina header include the OpenGL ES 3.2
`GLES3/gl32.h` header instead of the regular OpenGL header.

@anchor LUM_INCLUDE_NONE
__LUM_INCLUDE_NONE__ makes the Lumina header not include any OpenGL or OpenGL ES
API header.  This is useful in combination with an extension loading library.

If none of the above inclusion macros are defined, the standard OpenGL `GL/gl.h`
header (`OpenGL/gl.h` on macOS) is included, unless Lumina detects the inclusion
guards of any OpenGL, OpenGL ES or extension loader header it knows about.

The following macros control the inclusion of additional API headers.  Any
number of these may be defined simultaneously, and/or together with one of the
above macros.

@anchor LUM_INCLUDE_VULKAN
__LUM_INCLUDE_VULKAN__ makes the Lumina header include the Vulkan
`vulkan/vulkan.h` header in addition to any selected OpenGL or OpenGL ES header.

@anchor LUM_INCLUDE_GLEXT
__LUM_INCLUDE_GLEXT__ makes the Lumina header include the appropriate extension
header for the OpenGL or OpenGL ES header selected above after and in addition
to that header.

@anchor LUM_INCLUDE_GLU
__LUM_INCLUDE_GLU__ makes the header include the GLU header in addition to the
header selected above.  This should only be used with the standard OpenGL header
and only for compatibility with legacy code.  GLU has been deprecated and should
not be used in new code.

@note None of these macros may be defined during the compilation of Lumina itself.
If your build includes Lumina and you define any these in your build files, make
sure they are not applied to the Lumina sources.


@section build_link Link with the right libraries

Lumina is essentially a wrapper of various platform-specific APIs and therefore
needs to link against many different system libraries.  If you are using Lumina as
a shared library / dynamic library / DLL then it takes care of these links.
However, if you are using Lumina as a static library then your executable will
need to link against these libraries.

On Windows and macOS, the list of system libraries is static and can be
hard-coded into your build environment.  See the section for your development
environment below.  On Linux and other Unix-like operating systems, the list
varies but can be retrieved in various ways as described below.

A good general introduction to linking is
[Beginner's Guide to Linkers](https://www.lurklurk.org/linkers/linkers.html) by
David Drysdale.


@subsection build_link_win32 With MinGW or Visual C++ on Windows

The static version of the Lumina library is named `lumina`.  When using this
version, it is also necessary to link with some libraries that Lumina uses.

When using MinGW to link an application with the static version of Lumina, you
must also explicitly link with `gdi32`. Other toolchains including MinGW-w64
include it in the set of default libraries along with other dependencies like
`user32` and `kernel32`.

The link library for the Lumina DLL is named `luminadll`.  When compiling an
application that uses the DLL version of Lumina, you need to define the @ref
LUM_DLL macro _before_ any inclusion of the Lumina header.  This can be done
either with a compiler switch or by defining it in your source code.


@subsection build_link_cmake_source With CMake and Lumina source

This section is about using CMake to compile and link Lumina along with your
application.  If you want to use an installed binary instead, see @ref
build_link_cmake_package.

With a few changes to your `CMakeLists.txt` you can have the Lumina source tree
built along with your application.

Add the root directory of the Lumina source tree to your project.  This will add
the `lumina` target to your project.

@code{.cmake}
add_subdirectory(path/to/lumina)
@endcode

Once Lumina has been added, link your application against the `lumina` target.
This adds the Lumina library and its link-time dependencies as it is currently
configured, the include directory for the Lumina header and, when applicable, the
@ref LUM_DLL macro.

@code{.cmake}
target_link_libraries(myapp lumina)
@endcode

Note that the `lumina` target does not depend on OpenGL, as Lumina loads any OpenGL,
OpenGL ES or Vulkan libraries it needs at runtime.  If your application calls
OpenGL directly, instead of using a modern
[extension loader library](@ref context_glext_auto), use the OpenGL CMake
package.

@code{.cmake}
find_package(OpenGL REQUIRED)
@endcode

If OpenGL is found, the `OpenGL::GL` target is added to your project, containing
library and include directory paths.  Link against this like any other library.

@code{.cmake}
target_link_libraries(myapp OpenGL::GL)
@endcode

For a minimal example of a program and Lumina sources built with CMake, see the
[Lumina CMake Starter](https://github.com/juliettef/GLFW-CMake-starter) on GitHub.


@subsection build_link_cmake_package With CMake and installed Lumina binaries

This section is about using CMake to link Lumina after it has been built and
installed.  If you want to build it along with your application instead, see
@ref build_link_cmake_source.

With a few changes to your `CMakeLists.txt` you can locate the package and
target files generated when Lumina is installed.

@code{.cmake}
find_package(lumina 3.4 REQUIRED)
@endcode

Once Lumina has been added to the project, link against it with the `lumina` target.
This adds the Lumina library and its link-time dependencies, the include directory
for the Lumina header and, when applicable, the @ref LUM_DLL macro.

@code{.cmake}
target_link_libraries(myapp lumina)
@endcode

Note that the `lumina` target does not depend on OpenGL, as Lumina loads any OpenGL,
OpenGL ES or Vulkan libraries it needs at runtime.  If your application calls
OpenGL directly, instead of using a modern
[extension loader library](@ref context_glext_auto), use the OpenGL CMake
package.

@code{.cmake}
find_package(OpenGL REQUIRED)
@endcode

If OpenGL is found, the `OpenGL::GL` target is added to your project, containing
library and include directory paths.  Link against this like any other library.

@code{.cmake}
target_link_libraries(myapp OpenGL::GL)
@endcode


@subsection build_link_pkgconfig With makefiles and pkg-config on Unix

Lumina supports [pkg-config](https://www.freedesktop.org/wiki/Software/pkg-config/),
and the `lumina.pc` pkg-config file is generated when the Lumina library is built
and is installed along with it.  A pkg-config file describes all necessary
compile-time and link-time flags and dependencies needed to use a library.  When
they are updated or if they differ between systems, you will get the correct
ones automatically.

A typical compile and link command-line when using the static version of the
Lumina library may look like this:

@code{.sh}
cc $(pkg-config --cflags lumina) -o myprog myprog.c $(pkg-config --static --libs lumina)
@endcode

If you are using the shared version of the Lumina library, omit the `--static`
flag.

@code{.sh}
cc $(pkg-config --cflags lumina) -o myprog myprog.c $(pkg-config --libs lumina)
@endcode

You can also use the `lumina.pc` file without installing it first, by using the
`PKG_CONFIG_PATH` environment variable.

@code{.sh}
env PKG_CONFIG_PATH=path/to/lumina/src cc $(pkg-config --cflags lumina) -o myprog myprog.c $(pkg-config --libs lumina)
@endcode

The dependencies do not include OpenGL, as Lumina loads any OpenGL, OpenGL ES or
Vulkan libraries it needs at runtime.  If your application calls OpenGL
directly, instead of using a modern
[extension loader library](@ref context_glext_auto), you should add the `gl`
pkg-config package.

@code{.sh}
cc $(pkg-config --cflags lumina gl) -o myprog myprog.c $(pkg-config --libs lumina gl)
@endcode


@subsection build_link_xcode With Xcode on macOS

If you are using the dynamic library version of Lumina, add it to the project
dependencies.

If you are using the static library version of Lumina, add it and the Cocoa,
OpenGL and IOKit frameworks to the project as dependencies.  They can all be
found in `/System/Library/Frameworks`.


@subsection build_link_osx With command-line on macOS

It is recommended that you use [pkg-config](@ref build_link_pkgconfig) when
building from the command line on macOS.  That way you will get any new
dependencies added automatically.  If you still wish to build manually, you need
to add the required frameworks and libraries to your command-line yourself using
the `-l` and `-framework` switches.

If you are using the dynamic Lumina library, which is named `liblumina.3.dylib`, do:

@code{.sh}
cc -o myprog myprog.c -llumina -framework Cocoa -framework OpenGL -framework IOKit
@endcode

If you are using the static library, named `liblumina3.a`, substitute `-llumina3`
for `-llumina`.

Note that you do not add the `.framework` extension to a framework when linking
against it from the command-line.

@note Your machine may have `libGL.*.dylib` style OpenGL library, but that is
for the X Window System and will not work with the macOS native version of Lumina.

*/
